  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1)$ and $f(1)={e}^{2}.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{3}{4}).$",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(1)+{f}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(1)={e}^{2},$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=2{e}^{2x}x(x+1),$ then ${f}^{\u2032}(1)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 1}\\cdot 1(1+1)=4{e}^{2}.$ So, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(x-1).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{3}{4})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{3}{4}) & \u2248L(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}-1) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+4{e}^{2}(-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}-{e}^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
